{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey has no state rent control; municipalities may adopt rent control ordinances","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law that establishes, governs or controls rents. Municipalities may pass rent control or rent leveling ordinances, enforced by locally created boards.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rent regulation, where it exists, is municipal. Housing owned or subsidized by HUD and projects financed by the NJHMFA are not subject to municipal rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinances."},"exemptions":"Housing developments owned or subsidized by HUD, unsubsidized developments with HUD-insured mortgages having economic problems, and rents fixed and controlled by the NJHMFA are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling. Locally created boards enforce these ordinances.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in New Jersey; the 30-year exemption runs from completion of construction of the building.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Exempts covered buildings from municipal rent control ordinances.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rules on rent increases and unconscionable rent","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise rent mid-lease: the old lease must be terminated and a new lease offered at the increased rent, with notice to quit and rent-increase notice before any eviction for nonpayment of the increase. The increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies in New Jersey; an increase must also comply with any municipal rent control ordinance governing increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"This notice does not mean that the tenant must actually leave; the tenant has the right to remain as long as they pay any legal increase in rent. The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with municipal rent control ordinances where they exist.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Bar on rent increases to cover condominium or cooperative conversion costs","requirement":"If a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building so converted, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Rents may still be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement and notices","requirement":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease without good cause; each cause, except nonpayment of rent, must be described in detail in a written Notice to Quit, and a Judgment for Possession is required before the tenant must move.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; applies to written and oral leases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New owner's duties for a transferred security deposit","requirement":"When rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant’s security deposit plus accrued interest, and whether or not it is transferred is responsible for investing the deposit, giving all notices, paying interest and returning the deposit with accrued earnings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental security deposits when the property is sold or transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalty for willfully withholding deposits of tenants receiving government assistance","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit of a tenant receiving State or federal financial assistance, including rental assistance, may be liable for a civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 for each offense.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who received financial assistance through any State or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, collected and enforced by summary proceedings; the State entity that made the deposits is entitled to the penalty amounts. Unlawfully using security deposit monies may also be a disorderly persons offense with a fine of not less than $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination protection for source of lawful income in housing","requirement":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination prohibits discrimination in selling or renting housing, including on the basis of source of lawful income, and requires equal treatment in the rental of housing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in New Jersey, except the dwellings listed as exempt under the LAD.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex (YMCA and age-restricted housing), as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the LAD are subject to substantial fines and penalties, up to $10,000 for a first offense (N.J.S.A. 10:5-14.1a(a)).","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
